course: chinese-street
lesson: 28
title: At the friend's home: what to say to the parents
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The door opens and there are Ming's mum and dad. Before you look at a single toy you say «shūshu hǎo, āyí hǎo!» (hello Uncle, hello Auntie) — that's what children call a friend's parents, and two seconds of it wins them over.
explain: Then «qǐng jìn» (come in, please), and your eyes go to the row of slippers by the door: huàn tuōxié, change into slippers. Most Chinese homes are shoes-off, even if nobody says it.
explain: Inside you'll hear qǐng zuò (please sit), hē diǎnr shuǐ ba (have some water — often warm) and bié kèqi (don't be shy, make yourself at home). Take a little, say xièxie āyí, and you're doing brilliantly.
explain: Leaving has its own dance: you say wǒ gāi zǒu le, they ask you to stay a bit longer, you thank them, and at the door you hear màn zǒu — «walk slowly», which really means «get home safely».
fact: shūshu hǎo, āyí hǎo («SHOO-shoo HOW, AH-ee HOW») means hello Uncle, hello Auntie, said to the friend's dad and mum the moment you walk in; forgetting it is the biggest mistake.
fact: qǐng jìn («ching JIN») means come in, please, said by the host at the door; jìnlái is come in and chūqù is go out.
fact: huàn tuōxié («HWAHN TWAW-shyeh») means change into slippers; most Chinese homes are shoes-off, with slippers by the door for guests, and walking in with outdoor shoes is a real faux pas.
fact: qǐng zuò («ching DZWAW») means please sit down; wait to be shown where, because at the table the best seat is for the eldest.
fact: hē diǎnr shuǐ ba («HUH DYAR SHWAY ba») means have a drink of water, often warm water or tea; it is polite to accept a little.
fact: bié kèqi («BYEH KUH-chee», literally «don't be polite») means make yourself at home; it is an invitation, but still take one helping, not the whole plate.
fact: liú xiàlái chī fàn ba («LYO SHYAH-ligh CHR FAHN ba») means stay for dinner; the first invitation may only be polite, if they ask two or three times they mean it, and you ring home first: wǒ wèn yíxià wǒ māma.
fact: dǎrǎo le («DAH-RAOW luh») means sorry for disturbing you, said when leaving; it is the Chinese «thanks for having me», and parents are impressed when a child says it.
fact: wǒ gāi zǒu le («waw GUY DZOE luh») means I'd better go now; the hosts will politely ask you to stay (zài wánr yíhuìr ba), which is custom, and it is fine to go.
fact: màn zǒu («MAHN DZOE», literally «walk slowly») means take care going home; it is not telling you to dawdle.
fact: xià cì zài lái wánr («SHYAH TSZ DZYE LIGH WAR») means come and play again next time, the warmest goodbye, and a sign you did well; the answer is hǎo, xièxie shūshu āyí!
fact: zhè shì wǒ māma ràng wǒ dài de («JUH SHR waw MAH-ma RAHNG waw DIGH duh») means this is from my mum, said handing over a small gift like fruit on a first visit; they will protest «tài kèqi le!», and you keep holding it out with both hands until they take it.
exercise: You walk into Ming's flat and see his mum and dad. What do you say first? | shūshu hǎo, āyí hǎo | màn zǒu, màn zǒu | wǒ gāi zǒu le, bàibai | bié kèqi
exercise: Ming's dad opens the door: «qǐng jìn». What does he mean? | Come in, please | Go away now, please | Please sit down here | Please take your shoes
exercise: There's a row of slippers by Ming's door. What should you do? | Take off your shoes and put on slippers | Walk in with your trainers on | Put the slippers in your bag | Wait for someone to tell you twice
exercise: What does huàn tuōxié mean? | Change into slippers | Wash your hands first | Close the door behind you | Sit down on the floor
exercise: Ming's mum says qǐng zuò at the dinner table. What is wise? | Wait to be shown: the best seat is for the eldest | Grab the best chair quickly | Sit on the floor | Stay standing for the whole meal
exercise: «hē diǎnr shuǐ ba» What is Ming's mum offering? | A drink of water, maybe warm, or tea | A bowl of hot soup with noodles in it | A swim in the pool downstairs | A bath
exercise: What is polite when a drink is offered? | Accept a little and say xièxie āyí | Refuse three times, then leave | Ask for a can of cola instead | Say nothing
exercise: You hesitate over the dumplings and Ming's mum says «bié kèqi!». What does she mean? | Don't be shy, help yourself | Don't touch those, they're for later | Don't be rude | Don't eat too fast, you'll choke
exercise: What does bié kèqi literally mean? | Don't be polite | Don't come in yet | Don't be late again | Don't say thank you
exercise: Ming's mum says «liú xiàlái chī fàn ba». What is she inviting you to? | Stay for dinner | Stay the whole night | Leave before dinner | Help cook the dinner
exercise: She asks you to stay for dinner three times. What should you do? | Believe she means it and ring home first | Say no, it's only politeness | Stay without telling your mum at all | Leave at once
exercise: What is dǎrǎo le, said as you leave? | Sorry for disturbing you, thanks for having me | Hurry up, I'm going | I'm hungry again | See you tomorrow at school, then
exercise: How do you say «I'd better go now»? | wǒ gāi zǒu le | màn zǒu | qǐng jìn | qǐng zuò
exercise: Ming's dad says «màn zǒu» at the door. What does he mean? | Take care going home | Walk as slowly as you can | Hurry up and go home now | Come back inside again
exercise: The parents say «xià cì zài lái wánr!». What does it tell you? | You did well: come and play again | You stayed far too long this time, go home | Don't come back | Bring a better gift next time
exercise: You hold out a bag of oranges: «zhè shì wǒ māma ràng wǒ dài de». What are you saying? | This is from my mum, she asked me to bring it | My mum wants these oranges back by tonight, please | These are for my mum | My mum is waiting outside in the car
exercise: Ming's mum protests «tài kèqi le!» about your gift. What do you do? | Keep holding it out with both hands | Take it back home with you, because she said no | Throw it on the table | Eat it yourself, then
test: «didn't say hi to his parents, went straight to the PlayStation, it's fine right?» Is it fine? | No, not greeting the parents is the biggest mistake | Yes, Chinese parents don't expect a greeting from kids | Yes, if you say it at the end | No, you must bow three times
test: «nobody said take shoes off so I kept 'em on» What would have been better? | Take them off anyway and wear the slippers | Keep them on, nobody asked you, so it's totally fine | Take off your socks too | Carry your shoes in with you
test: Ming's mum asks once, lightly, if you want to stay for dinner, and doesn't ask again. What's the likely meaning? | It may just be politeness | She's angry with you | She means you must stay | She wants you to cook
test: «his dad said walk slowly?? I'm not a snail» What did màn zǒu mean? | Take care going home | Walk slowly, you're too noisy | You're being told off | Come back again later
test: You say «wǒ gāi zǒu le» and Ming's mum says «zài wánr yíhuìr ba». Do you have to stay? | No, it's custom to ask; it's fine to go | Yes, it's an order from a grown-up, so you stay | Yes, or she'll be insulted for ever | No, she means hurry up
test: What would happen if you said «dǎrǎo le» to Ming's parents on leaving? | They'd be impressed by such good manners | They'd think you broke something in the flat | They'd think you're staying the night | They'd think you're ill
test: «bié kèqi she said, so I ate the whole plate of dumplings lol» What went wrong? | It's an invitation, but take one helping | Nothing, that's exactly what bié kèqi means | bié kèqi means don't eat any | You should have eaten two plates
test: In a cartoon a boy hands Ming's mum a gift with one hand, she says «tài kèqi le» and he takes it back. What should he have done? | Kept offering it with both hands | Taken it back, she said no, so that's that | Thrown it to her | Given it to Ming instead, quietly
test: «it's freezing out, ming's mum gave me WARM water, weird??» What's going on? | Warm water or tea is normal in Chinese homes | She forgot to cool it down in the fridge first | It's a punishment | Warm water means it's time to go home
test: The parents say «xià cì zài lái wánr!» and you answer in silence. What's a lovely reply? | hǎo, xièxie shūshu āyí! | wǒ gāi zǒu le | qǐng jìn | bié kèqi
test: «can you stay for tea?» «dunno, gotta check» — how do you say «I'll ask my mum» at Ming's? | wǒ wèn yíxià wǒ māma | wǒ gāi zǒu le, xièxie | màn zǒu | dǎrǎo le, āyí, zàijiàn
exam: Identify the greeting customarily addressed by a visiting child to the parents of a friend. | shūshu hǎo, āyí hǎo | màn zǒu | dǎrǎo le | liú xiàlái chī fàn ba
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning footwear in a Chinese household. | Outdoor shoes are removed and the slippers provided are worn | Outdoor shoes are customarily retained throughout the entire visit | Guests are expected to bring their own slippers | Shoes are removed only in the presence of grandparents
exam: Ascertain the signification of the valediction màn zǒu, as uttered by a host. | A wish for the guest's safe journey home | An instruction to the guest to proceed at reduced speed | A request that the guest remain for dinner | A reproach for the guest's tardiness
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression dǎrǎo le, as uttered by a departing child? | A courteous apology for the disturbance, akin to «thank you for having me» | It is an admission of having damaged property belonging to the host family | It is a request for further refreshment | It is a greeting upon arrival
